1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,192
Remember the operator who blocked Aziz on Friday night?

2
00:00:03,242 --> 00:00:09,192
One search, one button, one reason, and every session of a person ended at once.

3
00:00:09,242 --> 00:00:11,256
That is a lot of power on one screen.

4
00:00:11,306 --> 00:00:15,192
So who may sit at that screen, and who watches the person who does?

5
00:00:15,242 --> 00:00:21,552
In Lesson 36, every staff action became an audit event in a chain that nobody can rewrite.

6
00:00:21,602 --> 00:00:29,976
This lesson opens the administrator panel itself: how staff sign in, what each role can do, and why no single person can change a rule alone.

7
00:00:30,026 --> 00:00:31,848
Start at the door.

8
00:00:31,898 --> 00:00:37,632
Staff sign in only through the bank's own staff identity, with multi-factor authentication there.

9
00:00:37,682 --> 00:00:42,480
The platform issues no staff credentials and keeps no staff passwords.

10
00:00:42,530 --> 00:00:48,696
A staff session ends after the configured time with no activity, and after the configured lifetime.

11
00:00:48,746 --> 00:00:51,120
Behind the door is the admin gateway.

12
00:00:51,170 --> 00:00:58,248
It uses the same domain code as the customer gateway, so there is no second copy of the business rules to drift apart.

13
00:00:58,298 --> 00:01:01,536
Each staff user has exactly one of four roles.

14
00:01:01,586 --> 00:01:07,344
The role comes from the bank's staff identity, or from a staff-role table that a security administrator keeps.

15
00:01:07,394 --> 00:01:15,840
A viewer can see the customer, the devices, the proofing history, the cases, the phone reviews and the events, and nothing more.

16
00:01:15,890 --> 00:01:27,120
An operator acts on customers: block and unblock, revoke devices, end sessions, reset attempt counters, ask for a refresh, and decide phone reviews and cases.

17
00:01:27,170 --> 00:01:33,528
An operator also cancels or approves a waiting password reset, and opens a closed app account again.

18
00:01:33,578 --> 00:01:40,944
A security administrator proposes and approves changes to policies and provider settings, and keeps the staff-role table.

19
00:01:40,994 --> 00:01:49,464
Compliance manages the texts of consents, terms and notifications, and exports the audit records and the data of one customer.

20
00:01:49,514 --> 00:01:56,136
Notice what is missing: the security administrator cannot block Aziz, and the operator cannot change a policy.

21
00:01:56,186 --> 00:02:02,352
And the platform checks the role at each operation on the server, not only by hiding a button on the screen.

22
00:02:02,402 --> 00:02:03,888
Now the operator's screen.

23
00:02:03,938 --> 00:02:10,536
The operator finds a customer only with the exact verified phone number, or the exact personal identification number.

24
00:02:10,586 --> 00:02:15,384
There is no search by name, so nobody browses customers out of curiosity.

25
00:02:15,434 --> 00:02:24,576
One screen shows the state, the level, the link, the devices, the app accounts with their sessions, the proofing history, the cases and the recent events.

26
00:02:24,626 --> 00:02:34,032
Sensitive values are masked: the personal identification number shows its first two and last two digits, and the document only its last four characters.

27
00:02:34,082 --> 00:02:39,120
The panel shows no full document numbers, no addresses and no images.

28
00:02:39,170 --> 00:02:41,832
Each operator action needs a written reason.

29
00:02:41,882 --> 00:02:49,680
It starts at once, it writes an audit event with the operator's identity, and when it touches the customer, it sends the security notification.

30
00:02:49,730 --> 00:02:55,920
Cases from Lesson 34 wait in one list, and phone reviews from Lesson 18 wait in another.

31
00:02:55,970 --> 00:03:02,712
Now the most important rule of the panel, and I think the one that saves a bank on a bad day: maker-checker.

32
00:03:02,762 --> 00:03:08,256
On Monday, a security administrator wants a shorter lifetime for one-time codes.

33
00:03:08,306 --> 00:03:09,912
The change does not happen.

34
00:03:09,962 --> 00:03:14,328
It becomes a proposal, which shows the current value next to the proposed one.

35
00:03:14,378 --> 00:03:17,064
A second security administrator opens it.

36
00:03:17,114 --> 00:03:21,600
The person who proposed cannot approve, so it must be someone else with the same role.

37
00:03:21,650 --> 00:03:25,320
The second person approves, and only then does the change start.

38
00:03:25,370 --> 00:03:30,816
It becomes a new version, with the proposer, the approver, the times and the reason.

39
00:03:30,866 --> 00:03:37,776
A rejected proposal is kept too, with its reason, and anyone with the role can propose an earlier version again.

40
00:03:37,826 --> 00:03:45,048
The same rule covers provider settings, consent and terms texts, notification templates, and the staff-role table.

41
00:03:45,098 --> 00:03:50,712
One tired person, or one stolen staff account, cannot quietly loosen a limit.

42
00:03:50,762 --> 00:03:55,608
To recap: staff come in through the bank's own identity, with one role each.

43
00:03:55,658 --> 00:04:03,672
Operators act on customers, security administrators on rules, Compliance on texts and exports, and viewers only look.

44
00:04:03,722 --> 00:04:09,240
Every action has a reason and an audit event, and every rule change needs two people.

45
00:04:09,290 --> 00:04:12,312
But everything in this panel is about real customers.

46
00:04:12,362 --> 00:04:17,184
Where does the team try all of it, again and again, without a real person's identity?

47
00:04:17,234 --> 00:04:19,344
Next: test identities.
